      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)

Use "lit issues [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== lit issues create --help
Create issues atomically.
Repeat the item boundary flag to submit an atomic batch, or use --file with a JSON array.
Scalar fields may occur once per item; repeated set fields preserve input order.

Requirements:
  Required: --issue TITLE for each flag-defined item, or --file PATH (nonempty JSON array).
  Do not mix --file with item flags. Repeat the boundary flag before each item's fields.
  Content is optional. --content and --content-file are mutually exclusive per item; stdin (-) may be read only once.
  New issues require project selection: --project REF, the session's selected project,
  or a project field in each JSON item.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.
  Issue titles need project selection: --project REF or the session's selected project.
  Issue UUIDs and project-qualified issue references can identify existing issues without that selection.

References:
  These rules apply to existing issue references such as --parent, not the new --issue title.
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.
  Issue REF: title in the selected project, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for a literal title, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.
  PROJECT_TITLE:ISSUE_REF selects an issue in another project, for example
  feat/api:title:Review API. A qualified prefix is scoped to that project;
  an unqualified UUID or ID prefix is global even when a project is selected.
  For titles containing slashes and colons, quote the argument and force title matching:
  --project test/poc 'title:Refactor a/b: cleanup'.

Valid forms:
  lit issues create --issue TITLE [--content TEXT | --content-file PATH]
  lit issues create --issue TITLE [ITEM_FLAGS] --issue TITLE [ITEM_FLAGS]
  lit issues create --file PATH
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.
  FILTERS / ITEM_FLAGS / CHANGE_FLAGS refer to applicable flags below.

Usage:
  lit issues create [flags]

Examples:
  lit issues create --session my-session --project test/poc --issue 'Verify the PoC' --content 'Exercise the tracker'

Flags:
      --assignee NAME       Optional. Assignee NAME; list accepts none for unassigned
      --content TEXT        Optional. UTF-8 content TEXT; conflicts with --content-file
      --content-file PATH   Optional. Read UTF-8 content from PATH; - reads stdin once
      --file PATH           Conditional (see valid forms). Input PATH: mutation JSON array or typed query object; - reads stdin once
      --force               Optional. Explicitly override competing claim ownership where supported
  -h, --help                help for create
      --issue REF           Conditional (see valid forms). Issue REF (create: new title); repeat to begin another atomic item
      --label LABEL         Optional. Initial issue LABEL (repeatable)
      --parent REF          Optional. Parent issue REF; list accepts none for no parent
      --project REF         Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection
      --state STATE         Optional. Issue STATE: open or closed

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit issues get --help
Read issues and current properties.
With --format markdown, one complete returned record becomes YAML frontmatter
plus the exact original Markdown body. Duplicate selectors may return one record;
multiple returned records use a structured report. Export each separately for documents.
This is a read snapshot, not an import format: --content-file reads the entire file as body.

Requirements:
  Required: one or more REF operands.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.
  Issue titles need project selection: --project REF or the session's selected project.
  Issue UUIDs and project-qualified issue references can identify existing issues without that selection.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.
  Issue REF: title in the selected project, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for a literal title, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.
  PROJECT_TITLE:ISSUE_REF selects an issue in another project, for example
  feat/api:title:Review API. A qualified prefix is scoped to that project;
  an unqualified UUID or ID prefix is global even when a project is selected.
  For titles containing slashes and colons, quote the argument and force title matching:
  --project test/poc 'title:Refactor a/b: cleanup'.

Valid forms:
  lit issues get REF...
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit issues get REF... [flags]

Examples:
  lit issues get --session my-session --project test/poc 'Verify the PoC'

Flags:
  -h, --help          help for get
      --project REF   Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit issues list --help
List and filter issues.
Filters combine with AND; set filters may repeat. --all conflicts with --limit/--cursor.
Reuse the same filters with a returned cursor. --all does not widen project scope.

Requirements:
  Required: project selection (--project or saved session), or --all-projects.
  --all-projects conflicts with explicit --project; query files may supply project_id or all_projects.
  --milestone REF is project-scoped and cannot be combined with --all-projects.
  Choose flag filters or a typed JSON query object: cannot combine --file with query/filter flags.
  --all conflicts with --limit/--cursor; --all does not widen project scope.
  Reuse the same filters with --cursor. Filters combine with AND.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.
  Issue titles need project selection: --project REF or the session's selected project.
  Issue UUIDs and project-qualified issue references can identify existing issues without that selection.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.
  Issue REF: title in the selected project, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for a literal title, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.
  PROJECT_TITLE:ISSUE_REF selects an issue in another project, for example
  feat/api:title:Review API. A qualified prefix is scoped to that project;
  an unqualified UUID or ID prefix is global even when a project is selected.
  For titles containing slashes and colons, quote the argument and force title matching:
  --project test/poc 'title:Refactor a/b: cleanup'.

Valid forms:
  lit issues list [--project REF | --all-projects] [FILTERS] [--limit N] [--cursor TOKEN]
  lit issues list [--project REF | --all-projects] [FILTERS] --all
  lit issues list --file PATH
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.
  FILTERS / ITEM_FLAGS / CHANGE_FLAGS refer to applicable flags below.

Usage:
  lit issues list [flags]

Examples:
  lit issues list --session my-session --project test/poc --limit 20

Flags:
      --all                      Optional. Return all results as one bounded snapshot; conflicts with --limit/--cursor
      --all-projects             Optional. Search or list across projects instead of the selected project
      --assignee NAME            Optional. Assignee NAME; list accepts none for unassigned
      --blocked BOOL             Optional. Filter blocked issues: BOOL true or false
      --claimed BOOL             Optional. Filter active work claims: BOOL true or false
      --created-after RFC3339    Optional. Inclusive creation boundary RFC3339
      --created-before RFC3339   Optional. Exclusive creation boundary RFC3339
      --cursor TOKEN             Optional. Continue the same query with opaque TOKEN
      --direction DIRECTION      Optional. Sort DIRECTION: asc or desc
      --file PATH                Optional. Input PATH: mutation JSON array or typed query object; - reads stdin once
  -h, --help                     help for list
      --id UUID                  Optional. Match object UUID (repeatable)
      --labels-all LABEL         Optional. Require every specified LABEL (repeatable)
      --labels-any LABEL         Optional. Require any specified LABEL (repeatable)
      --labels-none LABEL        Optional. Exclude every specified LABEL (repeatable)
      --limit N                  Optional. Page size N (default 100, maximum 1000 unless configured)
      --milestone REF            Optional. Milestone REF whose members to include; requires project scope
      --owner-client-id UUID     Optional. Filter claim owner by client UUID
      --parent REF               Optional. Parent issue REF; list accepts none for no parent
      --project REF              Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection
  -q, --query TEXT               Optional. Case-insensitive content substring TEXT
      --sort FIELD               Optional. Sort FIELD: created-at, updated-at, title
      --state STATE              Optional. Issue STATE: open or closed
      --title TITLE              Optional. Object TITLE (exact title filter on list)
      --updated-after RFC3339    Optional. Inclusive update boundary RFC3339
      --updated-before RFC3339   Optional. Exclusive update boundary RFC3339

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit issues update --help
Update issues atomically.
Repeat the item boundary flag to submit an atomic batch, or use --file with a JSON array.
Scalar fields may occur once per item; repeated set fields preserve input order.

Requirements:
  Required: target(s) using one of the forms below.
  Choose one input mode: positional targets, boundary-flag groups, or --file PATH (JSON array).
  Do not mix --file with item flags or positional targets; grouped and positional targets cannot mix.
  Use change flags to set, add, remove or clear fields; unchanged values are safe no-ops. --content and --content-file are mutually exclusive per item; stdin (-) may be read only once.
  CHANGE_FLAGS are the set/add/remove/clear flags listed below (for example --content TEXT).
  Repeat the boundary flag before each item's fields; positional targets share one set of fields.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.
  Issue titles need project selection: --project REF or the session's selected project.
  Issue UUIDs and project-qualified issue references can identify existing issues without that selection.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.
  Issue REF: title in the selected project, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for a literal title, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.
  PROJECT_TITLE:ISSUE_REF selects an issue in another project, for example
  feat/api:title:Review API. A qualified prefix is scoped to that project;
  an unqualified UUID or ID prefix is global even when a project is selected.
  For titles containing slashes and colons, quote the argument and force title matching:
  --project test/poc 'title:Refactor a/b: cleanup'.

Valid forms:
  lit issues update REF... [CHANGE_FLAGS] [--revision N]
  lit issues update --issue REF [CHANGE_FLAGS] [--revision N] [--issue REF [CHANGE_FLAGS] [--revision N]]
  lit issues update --file PATH
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.
  FILTERS / ITEM_FLAGS / CHANGE_FLAGS refer to applicable flags below.

Usage:
  lit issues update [REF...] [flags]

Examples:
  lit issues update --session my-session --project test/poc 'Verify the PoC' --content 'Updated details'

Flags:
      --add-label LABEL      Optional. Add issue LABEL (repeatable)
      --assignee NAME        Optional. Assignee NAME; list accepts none for unassigned
      --clear FIELD          Optional. Clear FIELD (repeatable): content, labels, assignee, parent
      --content TEXT         Optional. UTF-8 content TEXT; conflicts with --content-file
      --content-file PATH    Optional. Read UTF-8 content from PATH; - reads stdin once
      --file PATH            Conditional (see valid forms). Input PATH: mutation JSON array or typed query object; - reads stdin once
      --force                Optional. Explicitly override competing claim ownership where supported
  -h, --help                 help for update
      --issue REF            Conditional (see valid forms). Issue REF (create: new title); repeat to begin another atomic item
      --parent REF           Optional. Parent issue REF; list accepts none for no parent
      --project REF          Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection
      --remove-label LABEL   Optional. Remove issue LABEL (repeatable)
      --revision N           Optional. Expected object revision N; reject stale mutations
      --state STATE          Optional. Issue STATE: open or closed
      --title TITLE          Optional. Object TITLE (exact title filter on list)

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
