      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit claims release --help
Release claims owned by this session.

Requirements:
  Required: one or more issue REF operands, or --all for this session's owned claims.
  --all cannot be combined with targets or --project.
  Issue titles need project selection: --project REF or the session's selected project.
  Issue UUIDs and project-qualified issue references can identify existing issues without that selection.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.
  Issue REF: title in the selected project, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for a literal title, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.
  PROJECT_TITLE:ISSUE_REF selects an issue in another project, for example
  feat/api:title:Review API. A qualified prefix is scoped to that project;
  an unqualified UUID or ID prefix is global even when a project is selected.
  For titles containing slashes and colons, quote the argument and force title matching:
  --project test/poc 'title:Refactor a/b: cleanup'.

Valid forms:
  lit claims release REF... [--project REF]
  lit claims release --all
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit claims release [REF...] [flags]

Examples:
  lit claims release --session my-session --project test/poc 'Verify the PoC'

Flags:
      --all           Optional. Release all claims owned by this session; conflicts with explicit targets and --project
  -h, --help          help for release
      --project REF   Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit transactions --help
Reconcile uncertain mutation outcomes

Usage:
  lit transactions [flags]
  lit transactions [command]

Available Commands:
  status      Inspect retained requests without replaying them

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for transactions

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)

Use "lit transactions [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== lit transactions status --help
Inspect retained requests without replaying them.
Without flags, inspect all retained requests. --session filters by service/client binding.
Outcomes: committed, already_satisfied, uncommitted, or uncertain (exit 4). Never replays requests.

Requirements:
  Required flags: none. Without --file, inspect all retained request files.
  --file selects one retained request envelope (not a mutation array or stdin).
  Only explicit --session filters by service/client binding; LIT_SESSION does not filter.
  Each retained request supplies its own endpoint; this command never replays mutations.

Valid forms:
  lit transactions status [--session NAME]
  lit transactions status --file PATH [--session NAME]
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit transactions status [flags]

Examples:
  lit transactions status --session my-session

Flags:
      --file PATH   Optional retained request envelope PATH (not stdin or a mutation array)
  -h, --help        help for status

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit grep --help
Search the selected project, or explicitly use --all-projects. Matching is case-insensitive by default.
PATTERN is a literal substring. With -E/--regex it is a Go RE2 regular expression (linear time; no backreferences
or lookarounds), matched line by line as grep does: ^ and $ match at each line, and no match crosses a newline.
Regex matching ignores case with RE2's simple case folding, so ss does not match ß there (literal grep matches it).
Use -- before patterns beginning with a dash; grep -- --help searches for literal --help.

Requirements:
  Required: one nonempty PATTERN and project selection, or --all-projects.
  --project and --all-projects are mutually exclusive. Omitted --project uses session selection.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.

Valid forms:
  lit grep [--project REF | --all-projects] [-E] [--limit N] [--cursor TOKEN] PATTERN
  lit grep [--project REF | --all-projects] -- --help
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit grep PATTERN [flags]

Examples:
  lit grep --session my-session 'renewal'
  lit grep --session my-session -E 'econn(refused|reset)' --all-projects -n -C 1
  lit grep --session my-session -- --help

Flags:
      --all-projects     Optional. Search or list across projects instead of the selected project
      --case-sensitive   Optional. Match case exactly (literal and regex)
  -C, --context N        Optional. Include N surrounding lines in search excerpts
      --cursor TOKEN     Optional. Continue the same query with opaque TOKEN
  -h, --help             help for grep
      --limit N          Optional. Page size N (default 100, maximum 1000 unless configured)
  -n, --n                Optional. Show line numbers in search excerpts
      --project REF      Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection
  -E, --regex            Optional. Match PATTERN as a Go RE2 regular expression, line by line

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit setup-skills --help
setup-skills --scope local|user|custom --agent codex|claude|both [--path PARENT]
Installs all embedded skills offline; never starts lit-server.
Output is a JSON installation receipt; --format does not apply.
local: current directory. user: home directory. custom: --path is the parent of .codex/.claude.
--path is required only for custom scope. Existing owned files upgrade; local edits and collisions fail.
Both agents are preflighted before writes; unexpected errors can leave one host completed. Rerun safely.

Requirements:
  Required flags: --scope and --agent.
  --path is required with custom scope and forbidden with local or user scope.
  No tracker session or service is needed.

Valid forms:
  lit setup-skills --scope local|user --agent codex|claude|both
  lit setup-skills --scope custom --path DIRECTORY --agent codex|claude|both
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit setup-skills [flags]

Examples:
  lit setup-skills --scope local --agent both
  lit setup-skills --scope custom --path /work/project --agent codex

Flags:
      --agent AGENT      Required Target AGENT: codex, claude or both
  -h, --help             help for setup-skills
      --path DIRECTORY   Required with --scope custom; forbidden otherwise. Parent DIRECTORY of .codex/.claude for custom scope
      --scope SCOPE      Required Install SCOPE: local, user or custom
== lit tui --help
Open the terminal UI for people: the separate lit-tui executable.
lit looks for lit-tui next to its own executable first, then on PATH, and hands
the terminal over to it (on Unix lit is replaced by lit-tui). --endpoint is passed through.
Bare lit with no command opens it too, but only when stdin and stdout are terminals;
otherwise bare lit reports that a subcommand is required, so scripts are unaffected.
Install lit-tui with: go install github.com/nerdwave-nick/lit/cmd/lit-tui@latest

Requirements:
  Required flags: none. No session is needed; lit-tui must be installed next to lit or on PATH.
  lit tui needs an interactive terminal; lit-tui refuses to start without one.

Valid forms:
  lit tui [--endpoint URL]
  lit [--endpoint URL]  # on a terminal
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit tui [flags]

Examples:
  lit tui
  lit tui --endpoint http://127.0.0.1:7411

Flags:
      --endpoint URL   Optional. Service URL passed to lit-tui; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > http://127.0.0.1:7411
  -h, --help           help for tui
== lit search --help
Search the selected project, or explicitly use --all-projects, for the most relevant issues,
projects and milestones first. An issue's result includes its matching comments.
Words match whole words, ignoring case and diacritics (cafe finds Café); all words must match.
"a phrase" matches words in order, -word excludes, a OR b matches either, and word* matches prefixes.
Everything else is plain text. Titles count most, then bodies and descriptions, then comments.
Use -- before a query beginning with a dash. For literal substrings, use lit grep.

Requirements:
  Required: one nonempty QUERY and project selection, or --all-projects.
  --project and --all-projects are mutually exclusive. Omitted --project uses session selection.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.

Valid forms:
  lit search [--project REF | --all-projects] [--state open|closed] [--limit N] [--cursor TOKEN] QUERY
  lit search [--project REF | --all-projects] -- '-flaky timeout'
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit search QUERY [flags]

Examples:
  lit search --session my-session 'connection timeout'
  lit search --session my-session --all-projects --state open '"claim renewal" -flaky'

Flags:
      --all-projects   Optional. Search or list across projects instead of the selected project
      --cursor TOKEN   Optional. Continue the same query with opaque TOKEN
  -h, --help           help for search
      --limit N        Optional. Page size N (default 20, maximum 100)
      --project REF    Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection
      --state STATE    Optional. Only issues in STATE: open or closed

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit workflow-session --help
Bind explicit runtime identity to durable workflow state. Never starts a service.
Fresh sessions and subagents get isolated clients; resume reuses the recorded client.
Lifecycle commands output a JSON association record. run forwards tracker output with --format json;
--format does not apply to this adapter.

Usage:
  lit workflow-session [flags]
  lit workflow-session [command]

Available Commands:
  checkout    Record a repository checkout path
  fresh       Create an isolated logical client
  reconcile   Inspect a pending session binding
  resume      Resume the recorded client
  run         Run a tracker command with the bound client
  subagent    Create an isolated child client

Flags:
      --cli EXECUTABLE   Optional. Path to lit EXECUTABLE (default: this binary)
  -h, --help             help for workflow-session
      --host HOST        Required agent HOST: codex or claude
      --runtime-id ID    Required explicit vendor runtime session ID

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL   Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411

Use "lit workflow-session [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== lit workflow-session fresh --help
Create an isolated logical client.
Output is a JSON association record; --format does not apply.

Requirements:
  Required flags: --host and --runtime-id.
  Endpoint: --endpoint > LIT_ENDPOINT > http://127.0.0.1:7411.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.

Valid forms:
  lit workflow-session --host codex|claude --runtime-id ID fresh [--endpoint URL] [--project REF] [--discussion-id ID]
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit workflow-session fresh [flags]

Examples:
  lit workflow-session --host codex --runtime-id SESSION fresh

Flags:
      --discussion-id ID   Optional. Optional discussion ID for workflow metadata
  -h, --help               help for fresh
      --project REF        Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection

Global Flags:
      --cli EXECUTABLE   Optional. Path to lit EXECUTABLE (default: this binary)
      --endpoint URL     Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --host HOST        Required agent HOST: codex or claude
      --runtime-id ID    Required explicit vendor runtime session ID
== lit workflow-session subagent --help
Create an isolated child client.
Output is a JSON association record; --format does not apply.

Requirements:
  Required flags: --host, --runtime-id and --parent-runtime-id.
  The child runtime ID must be distinct from the parent runtime ID.
  Endpoint: --endpoint > LIT_ENDPOINT > http://127.0.0.1:7411.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.

Valid forms:
  lit workflow-session --host codex|claude --runtime-id ID subagent --parent-runtime-id PARENT [--endpoint URL] [--project REF] [--discussion-id ID]
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit workflow-session subagent [flags]

Examples:
  lit workflow-session --host codex --runtime-id SESSION subagent --parent-runtime-id PARENT

Flags:
      --discussion-id ID       Optional. Optional discussion ID for workflow metadata
  -h, --help                   help for subagent
      --parent-runtime-id ID   Required parent runtime session ID
      --project REF            Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection

Global Flags:
      --cli EXECUTABLE   Optional. Path to lit EXECUTABLE (default: this binary)
      --endpoint URL     Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --host HOST        Required agent HOST: codex or claude
      --runtime-id ID    Required explicit vendor runtime session ID
